Let's call the points of tangency $P$ and $Q$. Let $OP$ and $OQ$ be the perpendicular distances from $(0,5)$ to the tangent lines. We can find these distances by using the distance formula, which is $(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2$.

The circle centered at $(-4,0)$ can be parametrized as $(-4 + 1\cos t, 0 + 1\sin t)$, for $0 \leq t < 2\pi$. Its tangent line at the tangency point $Q$, namely when $t = t_Q$, will have the perpendicular distance $AP = OP - OP_0$, where

\begin{aligned}
OP_0 &= \sqrt{((0-4)^2+(5-0)^2)}\\
&= \sqrt{64 + 25} = \boxed{10} \text{ units}
\end{aligned}

Similarly, the circle centered at $(4,0)$ can be parametrized as $(4 + 1\cos t', 0 + 1\sin t')$, for $0 \leq t' < 2\pi$. Its tangent line at the tangency point $P$, namely when $t' = t_P$, will have the perpendicular distance $AQ = OQ - OQ_0$, where

\begin{aligned}
OQ_0 &= \sqrt{((0+4)^2+(5-0)^2)}\\
&= \sqrt{64 + 25} = \boxed{10} \text{ units}
\end{aligned}

The lines are tangent if and only if $AQ$ and $AP$ are equal. Therefore, our strategy is to set up the equality and isolate $t_Q - t_P$. Rewrite the equations for $OP_0$, $OQ_0$, and compute the equations for $AP$ and $AQ$.

\begin{aligned}
OP &= \sqrt{((0-(-4 + 1\cos t_Q))^2+(5-0)^2)} = AP_0\\
AP_0 &= \sqrt{((0-4 + 1\cos t_P)^2+(5-0)^2)}\\
OQ &= \sqrt{((0-4 - 1\cos t_Q)^2+(5-0)^2)} = AQ_0\\
AQ_0 &= \sqrt{((0-4 - 1\cos t_P)^2+(5-0)^2)}
\end{aligned}

The lines are equivalent when they intersect. Find the point where they intersect using another variable, $s$, which ranges from $0$ to $2\pi$ as well. Using these four coordinate equations for $t_P$, $t_Q$, $s$, and the $x,y$ coordinates for points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0)$, find the system of equations for $t_P$, $t_Q$, and $s$.

\begin{aligned}
t_P &= 2\pi - s\\
t_Q &= s
\end{aligned}

Since the x-coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ match the center of the circle at $(4,0)$, we get the first equation for $s$, by matching the equation for $(4,0)$ with our parametrization for the circle centered at $(4,0)$.

\begin{align*}
4 + 1\cos t_P &= 4 + \cos s\\
\cos t_P &= \cos s + 3
\end{align*}

Now, we need to solve the system of equations for $t_P$ and $t_Q$ using the equations for $OP$, $OQ$, $AP$, $AQ$, and $\cos t_P$:

\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{((0-4 + 1\cos (2\pi - s))^2+(5-0))^2} &= \sqrt{((0-4 + 1\cos s)^2+(5-0))^2} \\
\underbrace{(\cos (2\pi - s) + 3)}_{\text{cube}} - \expval{(\boxed{10})}{2} &= (\cos s + 3)^2 - \expval{(\boxed{10})}{2} \tag{temporary equation 1}
\end{aligned}

Simplify equation 1

\begin{align*}
\underbrace{(\cos s + 3)}_{\text{cube}}^2 + (2\cdot\expval{(\boxed{10})}{2}) - \underbrace{(\cos (2\pi) + 3)}_{\text{cube}} &= \cos^2s + 6\cos s + 9
\end{align*}

The second equation is given by the condition $AP = AQ$,

\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{((0-4 + 1\cos (2\pi - s))^2+(5-0))^2} &= \sqrt{((0-4 - 1\cos s)^2+(5-0))^2}
\end{aligned}

Simplify it by squaring and cube it

\begin{align*}
\underbrace{(\cos (2\pi - s) + 3)}_{\text{cube}} - \expval{(\boxed{10})}{2} &= \cos^2s + 6\cos s + 9 - (\cos (2\pi) + 3)
\end{align*}

Combine the simplified formulas for $OP = AQ$

\begin{align*}
&(\cos (2\pi - s) + 3)^2 - 2\cdot\expval{(\boxed{10})}{2} = \cos^2s + 6\cos s + 9 - \cos (2\pi - s)\\
&(\cos (2\pi - s) + 3) (\cos (2\pi - s) + 3) - 2\cdot\expval{(\boxed{10})}{2} = (\cos s)^2 + 6\cos s + 9 - \cos (2\pi - s)
\end{align*}

Multiply across and distribute

\begin{align*}
\cos^2(2\pi - s) + 3\cos (2\pi - s) + (\overbrace{\cos^2(2\pi - s)}^{\text{non-negative}}) + 3\cdot 3 - 2\cdot\expval{(\boxed{10})}{2} &= \cos^2s + 6\cos s + 9 - \cos (2\pi - s)
\end{align*}

Simplify the equation

\begin{align*}
13 - 6\cos s &= 13 - \cos (2\pi - s)\\
6\cos s &= \cos (2\pi - s)
\end{align*}

Let $\cos s = x$, then $x^2 = 1-6^2/13$, which has one positive root at $x = \frac{7}{10}$. Set $s = \arccos(\frac{7}{10})$ to find $s$. Set one of $t_P$ or $t_Q$ equal to $s$ (e.g., $t_Q$) and use the equations for $t_P$ to find the other. In this case compute $\cos (2\pi - s) = -\cos s$.

\begin{aligned}
-x &= -\frac{7}{10}\\
s &= \arccos\left(-\frac{7}{10}\right)\\
(2\pi - s) &= \arccos\frac{7}{10}\\
t_P &= 2\pi - \arccos\frac{7}{10}\\
t_Q &= \arccos\frac{7}{10}
\end{aligned}

The answer is:

\boxed{$t_P = 2\pi - \arccos\frac{7}{10}$ and $t_Q = \arccos\frac{7}{10}$}

