First, let's express $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}]$ in terms of $m$. Since $\mathop{\text{lcm}}$ is the smallest positive integer that is a multiple of all numbers, we can use partial fraction decomposition:
\ Formula for lcm of two numbers $a, b$ such that $\gcd(a, b) = d$ is:
 \begin{align*}
   & \mathop{\text{lcm}}[a, b] = \frac{|a|\cdot|b|}{\gcd(a,b)} \cdot \frac{\gcd(a,b)}{|a|} + \frac{\gcd(a,b)}{|b|} \cdot |b| \\
   & \mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}] = \frac{|8m|\cdot|10^{10}|}{\gcd(8m,10^{10})}\cdot\frac{\gcd(8m,10^{10})}{|8m|} + \frac{\gcd(8m,10^{10})}{|10^{10}|}\cdot|10^{10}| \\
   & = 4\cdot\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}] \implies 4 \cdot \frac{|m|\cdot10^{10}}{\gcd(m,10^{10})} \cdot \frac{\gcd(m,10^{10})}{|m|} + \frac{\gcd(m,10^{10})}{10^{10}} = 4 \cdot \mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]
   \end{align*}
  Since both sides must be equal, we have:
  \begin{align*}
    & 4 \cdot \frac{|10^{10}|}{\gcd(10^{10},m)} + \frac{\gcd(m,10^{10})}{10^{10}} = 4 \cdot \mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}] \\
   \implies & 4+\frac{\gcd(m,10^{10})}{10^{10}} = \frac{4\cdot\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]}{10^{10}}
  \end{align*}
  Taking the 10th power on both sides:
  \begin{align*}
