Here's the solution using LaTeX:

First, let's find the total miles run by a girl in one day:
\begin{align*}
\text{Girl's Daily Mileage} &= 3 \; \text{miles} + 6 \; \text{miles} \\
&= 9 \; \text{miles}.
\end{align*}

Next, to find the number of days each girl must practice to meet or exceed the boys' daily mileage, we have:

\begin{align*}
\text{Boys' Daily Mileage} &= 5 \; \text{miles}, \\
\text{Days to Catch Boys'} &= \left\lceil \frac{\text{Girl's Total Mileage}}{\text{Boys' Daily Mileage}} \right\rceil
&= \left\lceil \frac{\text{Total Miles}}{\text{5 miles}} \right\rceil.
\end{align*}

Finally, to find the total miles each girl will have run by the time the coach orders the new equipment, we multiply the daily mileage by the number of days it takes to catch the boys.

\begin{align*}
\text{Girl's Total Miles} &= \text{Days to Catch Boys} \times \text{Girl's Daily Mileage} \\
&= \left\lceil \frac{\text{Total Miles}}{\text{5 miles}} \right\rceil \times 9 \; \text{miles}.


