Let's denote the standard basis vectors in three dimensions $e_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ and $e_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$. The subspace spanned by $e_2$ is the $y$-axis. To find the projector onto this subspace, we form the projection matrix $P$ such that for any vector $v$, we have $Pv$ as the $y$-component of $v$. In other words, $Pv$ is the vector $v$ after discarding its $x$-component.

So, for each vector $v = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}$ in R³, the resulting vector $Pv$ after projection is the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}$. Setting up the projector $P$, we have:

\begin{align*}
P = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
Since this is a projection matrix, it must be self-adjoint or symmetric. To verify this, let's take the transpose of $P$ and compare it to $P$ itself:

\begin{align*}
P^T = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} = P
\end{align*}
Thus, $P$ is indeed a self-adjoint matrix, proving that it is the correct projector onto the $y$-axis.

