First, we need to find the lateral surface area of the tube, which is the area of a circle with radius equal to the diameter of the tube (since it is a cylinder), and the diameter is twice the radius:

- The radius of the circle is half of the height of the tube:
  \begin{align*}\radius &= \frac{\height}{2} \\ &= \frac{6 \unittext{ centimeters}}{2} \\ &= \boxed{ \text{3 centimeters} } \end{align*}

- The diameter of the circle is twice the radius:
  \begin{align*}\diameter &= 2\radius \\ &= 2 \times \boxed{ \text{3 centimeters} }  \\ &= \boxed{ \text{6 centimeters} } \end{align*}

- Now we can calculate the lateral surface area of the cylinder by taking the product of the circumference of the circle (which is $2\pi\radius$) and the height of the tube:
