We know that the surface area of a cube is given by 6 times the square of the length of one side. Let the side length of the cube be denoted by x. We have:

Portion of the given equation where we have to substitute. \begin{align*}
x^2 \cdot 6 &= 0.027 \text{ cubic meters} \\
x^2 &= \frac{0.027}{6} \text{ cubic meters} \\
x &= \sqrt{\frac{0.027}{6}} \text{ meters}
\end{align*}
Next, we'll find the square of the side length to get the surface area:
\begin{align*}
\text{Surface Area} &= 6(x^2) \\
&= 6\left(\sqrt{\frac{0.027}{6}}^2\right) \text{(Since } \sqrt{a^2} = a\text{ for } a \geq 0) \\
&= 6\left(\frac{0.027}{6}\right) \\
&= \boxed{\frac{0.027}{1} \text{ square meters } }
\end{align*

completions_4:
First, we need to find the length of one edge of the cube (side). Let's denote it as $x$. The given volume is $0.027 \ \text{ cubic meters}$, so we can write the equation for the volume of a cube: $x^3 = 0.027 \ \text{ cubic meters}$. Solve for $x$:

\begin{align*}
x^3 &= 0.027 \\
x &= \sqrt[3]{0.027} \\
x &= 0.3441 \\
\end{align*}

Now, let's find the surface area of the cube: $6x^2$.

\begin{align*}
\text{Surface Area (SA)} &= 6 \times 0.3441^2 \\
\text{SA} &= 6 \times 0.1177 \\
\text{SA} &= 0.7062 \ \text{ square meters } \\
\end{align*}

So, the surface area of the cube is \boxed{0.7062 square meters}.

Again, just one line of output:

\boxed{0.7062 square meters}

